27:["$","div","What to do in an emergency",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-display text-lg font-semibold","children":"What to do in an emergency"}],[["$","p","For a serious emergency such as chest pain, seve",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-base","children":"For a serious emergency such as chest pain, severe bleeding, breathing difficulty, stroke symptoms or a road accident, call 112 or 108 immediately. Do not wait to search for a hospital while the patient’s condition worsens. If an ambulance is not available quickly, take the patient to the nearest hospital with an emergency department."}],["$","p","Carry the patient’s ID, any medical records and ",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground md:text-base","children":"Carry the patient’s ID, any medical records and a list of current medicines. Tell the staff about allergies and existing conditions. Most hospitals must provide initial emergency care regardless of payment."}]],["$","ul",null,{"className":"list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","li","Emergency numbers: 112 for all emergencies, 108 for ambulance in many states",{"children":"Emergency numbers: 112 for all emergencies, 108 for ambulance in many states"}],["$","li","Keep a list of nearby hospitals saved on your phone",{"children":"Keep a list of nearby hospitals saved on your phone"}],["$","li","Note the hospital’s emergency entrance on the map",{"children":"Note the hospital’s emergency entrance on the map"}]]}],null]}]
